# React Memoization Patterns

## Overview
Memoization techniques in React prevent unnecessary re-renders and improve performance by caching computed values and function references.

## React.memo() - Component Memoization

### Basic Usage
Prevents a component from re-rendering if its props haven't changed:

```typescript
const ProductCard = React.memo(({ product, onAddToCart }: Props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{product.name}</h3>
      <button onClick={() => onAddToCart(product.id)}>Add to Cart</button>
    </div>
  );
});

export default ProductCard;
```

### Custom Comparison Function
Provide custom logic to determine if props have changed:

```typescript
const ProductCard = React.memo(
  (props) => <div>{props.product.name}</div>,
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    // Return true if props are EQUAL (skip re-render)
    // Return false if props are DIFFERENT (do re-render)
    return prevProps.product.id === nextProps.product.id &&
           prevProps.isInCart === nextProps.isInCart;
  }
);
```

### With Function Props
When passing functions as props, use useCallback() to maintain referential equality:

```typescript
export const ProductList = ({ products }: Props) => {
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((productId: string) => {
    setCartItems(prev => [...prev, productId]);
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      {products.map(product => (
        <ProductCard
          key={product.id}
          product={product}
          onAddToCart={handleAddToCart}
        />
      ))}
    </>
  );
};

const ProductCard = React.memo(ProductCardComponent);
```

## useMemo Hook - Value Memoization

### Purpose
Cache expensive computations and only recalculate when dependencies change.

### Basic Pattern
```typescript
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
  // This computation only runs if dependencies change
  return computeExpensiveValue(data);
}, [data]); // Only recalculate when 'data' changes
```

### Filtering and Sorting Example
```typescript
const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');
const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState('name');

// Memoized filtered and sorted products
const processedProducts = useMemo(() => {
  let result = [...products]; // Copy array to avoid mutation

  // Filter
  if (searchTerm) {
    result = result.filter(p =>
      p.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    );
  }

  // Sort
  if (sortBy === 'price') {
    result.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
  } else if (sortBy === 'rating') {
    result.sort((a, b) => b.rating - a.rating);
  }

  return result;
}, [products, searchTerm, sortBy]); // Recalculate only when these change
```

### Processing API Responses
```typescript
const processedReviews = useMemo(() => {
  return reviews.reduce((acc, review) => {
    if (!acc[review.productId]) {
      acc[review.productId] = 0;
    }
    acc[review.productId]++;
    return acc;
  }, {} as Record<string, number>);
}, [reviews]);
```

## useCallback Hook - Function Memoization

### Purpose
Maintain referential equality for function props so memoized children don't re-render unnecessarily.

### Basic Pattern
```typescript
const memoizedCallback = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(a, b);
}, [a, b]); // Function is recreated only if a or b change
```

### Event Handler Example
```typescript
const ProductList = ({ products, onCartUpdate }: Props) => {
  // Without useCallback, this function is recreated on every render
  // causing ProductCard (if memoized) to re-render unnecessarily
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((productId: string) => {
    setCartItems(prev => [...prev, productId]);
    onCartUpdate?.(productId);
  }, [onCartUpdate]); // Only recreate if onCartUpdate changes

  return (
    <div>
      {products.map(product => (
        <ProductCard
          key={product.id}
          product={product}
          onAddToCart={handleAddToCart}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};
```

## Combined Pattern: React.memo + useCallback

### Full Example
```typescript
// Parent Component
export const ProductList = React.memo(({ products, reviews }: Props) => {
  const [cartItems, setCartItems] = useState<string[]>([]);
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');

  // Memoize filtered products
  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {
    return products.filter(p =>
      p.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    );
  }, [products, searchTerm]);

  // Memoize add to cart handler
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((productId: string) => {
    setCartItems(prev => [...prev, productId]);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        placeholder="Search products..."
        value={searchTerm}
        onChange={e => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}
      />
      {filteredProducts.map(product => (
        <MemoizedProductCard
          key={product.id}
          product={product}
          isInCart={cartItems.includes(product.id)}
          onAddToCart={handleAddToCart}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
});

// Child Component - Memoized to prevent unnecessary re-renders
const ProductCard = ({ product, isInCart, onAddToCart }: Props) => {
  performance.mark(`ProductCard-render-${product.id}`);

  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{product.name}</h3>
      <button
        onClick={() => onAddToCart(product.id)}
        disabled={isInCart}
      >
        {isInCart ? 'In Cart' : 'Add to Cart'}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};

const MemoizedProductCard = React.memo(ProductCard, (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  // Return true if props are equal (skip re-render)
  return (
    prevProps.product.id === nextProps.product.id &&
    prevProps.isInCart === nextProps.isInCart &&
    prevProps.onAddToCart === nextProps.onAddToCart
  );
});
```

## When to Use Each

| Technique | When | Why |
|-----------|------|-----|
| `React.memo()` | Component receives same props from parent | Prevents re-render of child when parent re-renders |
| `useMemo()` | Computing a value is expensive | Avoids recalculating on every render |
| `useCallback()` | Passing functions to memoized children | Maintains referential equality |

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

### ❌ Unnecessary Memoization
```typescript
// Don't memoize simple operations
const doubled = useMemo(() => count * 2, [count]);
```

### ✅ Correct - Only memoize expensive operations
```typescript
const sortedData = useMemo(() => {
  return largeArray.sort((a, b) => a.value - b.value);
}, [largeArray]);
```

### ❌ Missing Dependencies
```typescript
// Bad - function uses searchTerm but doesn't list it as dependency
const handleSearch = useCallback(() => {
  filters.search = searchTerm; // Bug: stale closure
}, []); // Missing searchTerm dependency
```

### ✅ Correct - Include all dependencies
```typescript
const handleSearch = useCallback(() => {
  filters.search = searchTerm;
}, [searchTerm]); // Included in dependency array
```

## Performance Trade-offs

- **Memoization has a cost**: Memory and comparison logic
- **Use for**: Components rendering lists, expensive calculations, frequently updated state
- **Avoid for**: Simple leaf components, cheap operations
- **Rule of thumb**: Memoize if the cost of memoization is less than the cost of re-rendering

## References
- React.memo documentation: https://react.dev/reference/react/memo
- useMemo hook: https://react.dev/reference/react/useMemo
- useCallback hook: https://react.dev/reference/react/useCallback

