# Bundle Size Reduction Techniques

## Overview
Reducing bundle size improves initial load time, Time to Interactive (TTI), and overall application performance.

## 1. Tree-Shaking and Selective Imports

### Lodash - Common Bundle Size Problem

#### ❌ Problem: Import Entire Library
```typescript
import _ from 'lodash';

// Uses only 2 functions but imports entire library (70KB)
const filtered = _.filter(products, p => p.active);
const counted = _.countBy(products, 'category');
```

#### ✅ Solution 1: Direct Path Imports
```typescript
// Import only what you need from lodash
import filter from 'lodash/filter';
import countBy from 'lodash/countBy';

const filtered = filter(products, p => p.active);
const counted = countBy(products, 'category');
```

#### ✅ Solution 2: Use Lighter Alternatives
```typescript
// Use native Array methods for simple operations
// Native filter - no library needed
const filtered = products.filter(p => p.active);

// Native reduce for counting
const counted = products.reduce((acc, p) => {
  acc[p.category] = (acc[p.category] || 0) + 1;
  return acc;
}, {});
```

#### ✅ Solution 3: Use lodash-es
```typescript
// lodash-es supports tree-shaking better than default lodash
import { filter, countBy } from 'lodash-es';

const filtered = filter(products, p => p.active);
const counted = countBy(products, 'category');
```

### MathJS - Complex Library Reduction

#### ❌ Problem: Import Entire MathJS
```typescript
import * as math from 'mathjs';

// Uses only basic statistical functions
const mean = math.mean(numbers);
const median = math.median(numbers);
const variance = math.variance(numbers);
```

#### ✅ Solution 1: Dynamic Import (Only When Needed)
```typescript
// Load mathjs only on the Compare page where it's used
const [stats, setStats] = useState(null);

useEffect(() => {
  (async () => {
    const math = await import('mathjs');
    setStats({
      mean: math.mean(numbers),
      median: math.median(numbers),
      variance: math.variance(numbers)
    });
  })();
}, []);
```

#### ✅ Solution 2: Implement Lightweight Alternatives
```typescript
// Implement simple statistical functions instead of importing mathjs
const calculateMean = (numbers: number[]): number => {
  return numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / numbers.length;
};

const calculateMedian = (numbers: number[]): number => {
  const sorted = [...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const mid = Math.floor(sorted.length / 2);
  return sorted.length % 2 !== 0 ? sorted[mid] : (sorted[mid - 1] + sorted[mid]) / 2;
};

const calculateVariance = (numbers: number[]): number => {
  const mean = calculateMean(numbers);
  const squaredDiffs = numbers.map(n => Math.pow(n - mean, 2));
  return calculateMean(squaredDiffs);
};
```

#### ✅ Solution 3: Pre-computed Statistics
```typescript
// If data doesn't change frequently, pre-compute stats
const STATISTICS = {
  mean: 45.5,
  median: 43.0,
  variance: 156.25,
  stdDev: 12.5
};

// Use pre-computed values instead of calculating
const displayStats = () => {
  return <div>Mean: {STATISTICS.mean}</div>;
};
```

## 2. Code Splitting and Dynamic Imports

### Page-Level Splitting

```typescript
// Automatically split routes in Next.js App Router
// Each page bundle is separate
src/app/page.tsx           // Homepage bundle
src/app/compare/page.tsx   // Compare page bundle
```

### Component-Level Dynamic Imports

```typescript
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Load heavy component only when needed
const HeavyChart = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyChart'), {
  loading: () => <div>Loading chart...</div>,
  ssr: false // Don't render on server if not needed
});

export default function Dashboard() {
  const [showChart, setShowChart] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowChart(!showChart)}>Toggle Chart</button>
      {showChart && <HeavyChart data={data} />}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## 3. Analyzing Bundle Size

### Using Next.js Bundle Analyzer

#### Installation
```bash
npm install --save-dev @next/bundle-analyzer
```

#### Configuration (next.config.js)
```javascript
const withBundleAnalyzer = require('@next/bundle-analyzer')({
  enabled: process.env.ANALYZE === 'true',
});

module.exports = withBundleAnalyzer({
  // Next.js config
});
```

#### Usage
```bash
ANALYZE=true npm run build
```

### Chrome DevTools Network Analysis

1. Open Chrome DevTools → Network tab
2. Set throttling to see realistic performance
3. Look for:
   - Large JS bundles
   - Unused CSS
   - Image sizes
   - Third-party scripts

## 4. Library Alternatives

### Replace Heavy Libraries

| Heavy Library | Lightweight Alternative | Savings |
|---|---|---|
| lodash (70KB) | lodash-es or native methods | 70-100% |
| moment.js (65KB) | date-fns (13KB) or dayjs (2KB) | 80-97% |
| mathjs (180KB) | Simple functions or ml-matrix | 80-95% |
| jQuery (86KB) | Vanilla JS or Alpine.js | 100% |
| axios (13KB) | Fetch API | 100% |

### Example: Date Formatting
```typescript
// ❌ Heavy: moment.js
import moment from 'moment';
const formatted = moment(date).format('YYYY-MM-DD');

// ✅ Lightweight: Native
const formatted = new Date(date).toISOString().split('T')[0];

// ✅ Lightweight: date-fns
import { format } from 'date-fns';
const formatted = format(date, 'yyyy-MM-dd');
```

## 5. CSS Bundle Reduction

### Tailwind CSS Purging

#### Configure in tailwind.config.js
```javascript
module.exports = {
  content: [
    './src/pages/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}',
    './src/components/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}',
    './src/app/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}',
  ],
  theme: {},
  plugins: [],
};
```

Only used Tailwind classes are included in the final CSS bundle.

### Remove Unused CSS
```typescript
// ❌ Don't load all Tailwind utilities
@import 'tailwindcss/base';
@import 'tailwindcss/components';
@import 'tailwindcss/utilities';
@import 'tailwindcss/plugins';

// ✅ Load only what you need
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;
```

## 6. Next.js Specific Optimizations

### Image Optimization

```typescript
// ❌ Unoptimized image
<img src="/product.jpg" alt="Product" />

// ✅ Optimized with Next.js Image
import Image from 'next/image';

<Image
  src="/product.jpg"
  alt="Product"
  width={200}
  height={200}
  placeholder="blur"
  priority={isAboveTheFold}
/>
```

Benefits:
- Automatic format conversion (WebP, AVIF)
- Responsive image sizes
- Lazy loading
- Blur placeholder

### ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration)

```typescript
// Don't use force-dynamic if possible
// Instead, use revalidate for caching

// ❌ Slow - Always fetch fresh data
export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';

// ✅ Fast - Cache for 60 seconds
export const revalidate = 60;
```

## 7. Performance Checklist

- [ ] Analyze bundle size with @next/bundle-analyzer
- [ ] Remove unused dependencies
- [ ] Replace heavy libraries with lightweight alternatives
- [ ] Use dynamic imports for non-critical code
- [ ] Optimize images with Next.js Image component
- [ ] Remove force-dynamic where possible
- [ ] Use selective imports instead of default exports
- [ ] Implement code splitting at route level
- [ ] Monitor bundle size in CI/CD pipeline

## References
- Next.js Optimization: https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/optimizing
- Bundle Analysis: https://web.dev/measure/
- Bundlephobia: https://bundlephobia.com/ (Compare library sizes)
- JavaScript Bundle Size Benchmarks: https://bundlephobia.com/

