# API Response Optimization Guide

## Overview
API response optimization focuses on reducing latency, minimizing data transfer, and batching requests to improve perceived performance.

## 1. Parallel vs Sequential API Requests

### Problem: Sequential Requests
Sequential API calls block each other, increasing total response time.

```typescript
// ❌ SLOW: Total time = T1 + T2 + T3
async function fetchData() {
  const user = await fetch('/api/user');
  const products = await fetch('/api/products');
  const reviews = await fetch('/api/reviews');

  return { user, products, reviews };
}
// If each request takes 200ms: 200 + 200 + 200 = 600ms total
```

### Solution: Parallel Requests
Use Promise.all() to fetch all data simultaneously.

```typescript
// ✅ FAST: Total time = MAX(T1, T2, T3)
async function fetchData() {
  const [userRes, productsRes, reviewsRes] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/user'),
    fetch('/api/products'),
    fetch('/api/reviews')
  ]);

  const [user, products, reviews] = await Promise.all([
    userRes.json(),
    productsRes.json(),
    reviewsRes.json()
  ]);

  return { user, products, reviews };
}
// If each request takes 200ms: 200ms total (parallel)
// 3x performance improvement!
```

## 2. API Request Batching

### Problem: Multiple Requests for Related Data
Separate API calls for related data create unnecessary overhead.

```typescript
// ❌ SLOW: 3 network requests
const user = await fetch('/api/user');
const products = await fetch('/api/products');
const reviews = await fetch('/api/reviews');
```

### Solution: Single Batched Request
Combine multiple requests into one endpoint.

```typescript
// ✅ FAST: 1 network request
const response = await fetch('/api/products'); // This endpoint returns all data
const { user, products, reviews } = await response.json();
```

### Backend Implementation

```typescript
// pages/api/products.ts
export async function GET(req: NextRequest) {
  const API_BASE = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE || 'http://localhost:3001';

  try {
    // Fetch all data in parallel
    const [userRes, productsRes, reviewsRes] = await Promise.all([
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/user`, { cache: 'no-store' }),
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/products`),
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/reviews`)
    ]);

    const user = await userRes.json();
    const products = await productsRes.json();
    const reviews = await reviewsRes.json();

    // Return all data in single response
    return NextResponse.json({
      user,
      products,
      reviews,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    });
  } catch (error) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Failed to fetch data' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

## 3. Response Caching

### Caching Strategies

#### Immutable Data (Products, Reviews)
```typescript
// Cache for 24 hours - data doesn't change frequently
fetch('/api/products', {
  next: { revalidate: 86400 } // 24 hours in seconds
});
```

#### Dynamic Data (User Profile)
```typescript
// Cache for 60 seconds - user data changes frequently
fetch('/api/user', {
  next: { revalidate: 60 } // 60 seconds
});
```

#### No Cache (Sensitive Data)
```typescript
// Don't cache - always fresh
fetch('/api/user/profile', {
  next: { revalidate: 0 } // No cache
  // or
  // cache: 'no-store'
});
```

### Cache Implementation in Next.js

```typescript
// pages/api/products.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export async function GET(req: NextRequest) {
  const API_BASE = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE || 'http://localhost:3001';

  try {
    // Parallel requests with different cache strategies
    const [userRes, productsRes, reviewsRes] = await Promise.all([
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/user`, {
        next: { revalidate: 60 } // Cache user for 60s
      }),
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/products`, {
        next: { revalidate: 3600 } // Cache products for 1 hour
      }),
      fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/reviews`, {
        next: { revalidate: 3600 } // Cache reviews for 1 hour
      })
    ]);

    const [user, products, reviews] = await Promise.all([
      userRes.json(),
      productsRes.json(),
      reviewsRes.json()
    ]);

    return NextResponse.json({ user, products, reviews });
  } catch (error) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Failed to fetch data' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

## 4. Response Size Optimization

### Selective Field Queries
Only fetch the fields you need.

```typescript
// ❌ SLOW: Fetch all fields
const response = await fetch('/api/products');
const { user, products, reviews } = await response.json();
// Returns: user name, email, address, phone, preferences... (unnecessary data)

// ✅ FAST: Fetch only needed fields
const response = await fetch('/api/products?fields=products,reviews');
// Returns only: products and reviews (minimal data transfer)
```

### Pagination
For large datasets, implement pagination.

```typescript
// Fetch only 20 items at a time
const response = await fetch('/api/products?page=1&limit=20');
```

### Compression
Enable gzip compression on the server.

```typescript
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  compress: true, // Enable compression by default in Next.js
};
```

## 5. Client-Side Optimization

### Request Deduplication
Avoid making the same request multiple times simultaneously.

```typescript
// Simple request deduplication cache
const requestCache = new Map<string, Promise<any>>();

export async function fetchCached(url: string) {
  if (!requestCache.has(url)) {
    requestCache.set(url, fetch(url).then(r => r.json()));
  }
  return requestCache.get(url);
}

// Usage: Both calls use the same cached promise
Promise.all([
  fetchCached('/api/products'),
  fetchCached('/api/products')
]); // Only 1 actual request made
```

### Client-Side Caching
Implement simple caching for frequently accessed data.

```typescript
interface CacheEntry<T> {
  data: T;
  timestamp: number;
  ttl: number;
}

const cache = new Map<string, CacheEntry<any>>();

export async function fetchWithCache<T>(
  url: string,
  ttl: number = 60000 // 60 seconds
): Promise<T> {
  const cached = cache.get(url);
  const now = Date.now();

  if (cached && now - cached.timestamp < cached.ttl) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  const data = await fetch(url).then(r => r.json());
  cache.set(url, { data, timestamp: now, ttl });
  return data;
}
```

## 6. Network Request Analysis

### Monitoring Performance

```typescript
// Measure API response times
const startTime = performance.now();
const response = await fetch('/api/products');
const endTime = performance.now();

console.log(`API response time: ${endTime - startTime}ms`);
```

### Identifying Slow Requests

1. Open Chrome DevTools → Network tab
2. Look for:
   - Large file sizes
   - Long response times (> 200ms)
   - Waterfall patterns showing sequential requests
   - Duplicate requests
3. Optimize the slowest requests first

## 7. Common Performance Patterns

### Parallel Data Fetching
```typescript
// ✅ Pattern: Fetch all data in parallel
async function getData() {
  const [userRes, productsRes, reviewsRes] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/user'),
    fetch('/api/products'),
    fetch('/api/reviews')
  ]);
  return {
    user: await userRes.json(),
    products: await productsRes.json(),
    reviews: await reviewsRes.json()
  };
}
```

### Progressive Enhancement
```typescript
// ✅ Pattern: Load critical data first, optional data later
async function getData() {
  // Critical: User and products
  const [userRes, productsRes] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/user'),
    fetch('/api/products')
  ]);

  const user = await userRes.json();
  const products = await productsRes.json();

  // Optional: Reviews loaded after page renders
  fetch('/api/reviews').then(r => r.json()).then(reviews => {
    updateReviewsOnPage(reviews);
  });

  return { user, products };
}
```

### Error Handling
```typescript
// ✅ Pattern: Graceful degradation on API failure
async function getData() {
  try {
    const [userRes, productsRes] = await Promise.all([
      fetch('/api/user'),
      fetch('/api/products')
    ]);

    if (!userRes.ok || !productsRes.ok) {
      throw new Error('API request failed');
    }

    return {
      user: await userRes.json(),
      products: await productsRes.json()
    };
  } catch (error) {
    // Return default data if API fails
    return {
      user: { name: 'Guest', id: 'guest' },
      products: []
    };
  }
}
```

## 8. Performance Checklist

- [ ] Use Promise.all() for parallel requests
- [ ] Batch related API calls into single endpoint
- [ ] Implement appropriate caching strategies
- [ ] Remove force-dynamic from page components
- [ ] Monitor API response times with performance.timing
- [ ] Reduce response payload sizes
- [ ] Enable compression on backend
- [ ] Implement request deduplication
- [ ] Handle errors gracefully

## References
- Fetch API: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API
- Promise.all(): https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all
- Next.js Data Fetching: https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/data-fetching
- Web Performance: https://web.dev/performance/

